---
/**
 * Who writes this.
 *
 * Written separately from Deep Creek's version, not adapted from it. The first
 * attempt was a find-and-replace and shared eighteen sentences with the other
 * site, which is the exact failure the voice audit flagged. Same person, two
 * different relationships to two different places, so the pages should have
 * nothing in common but the name.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    mainEntity: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: "Matt Werth",
      jobTitle: "Software engineer",
      worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Werth Design", url: "https://werth.design" },
      description: "Software engineer and photographer. Travels the US to see parks, and writes and checks the Delaware Field Guide.",
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Who writes this — Delaware Field Guide"
  description="Matt Werth writes and checks this site. Software engineer and photographer, north Wilmington, rings the town clerk before writing anything down."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">About</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[14ch] text-marsh">Who writes this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      I'm Matt Werth — a software engineer and photographer in north
      Wilmington. I spend a lot of time traveling with my family, visiting
      parks and beaches, and trying to answer the practical questions that come
      up along the way.
    </p>

    <h2>It started with the dog</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I wanted to know which Delaware beach he could walk on. I found three
      different answers, and two were out of date.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That sent me looking for something better: information that tells you
      when it was checked, who confirmed it, and where it came from.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      So that's what I do here. I call the person or agency responsible for the
      rule, record the date and name, and put that on the page. If I only find
      the answer on an official website, I say that instead. If something
      hasn't been checked, I mark it unchecked.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I'd rather have a shorter list of verified information than a bigger list
      of things I can't stand behind.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The photographs are mine. The day-to-day engineering work is mine too,
      through <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a> in
      Wilmington. Nothing here is sponsored at the moment.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      If I get something wrong, I want to know. Every page has a quick way to
      report a correction, and <a href="/changed/">changes are logged</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where AI fits in</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There's one AI feature on the site: <a href="/ask/">Ask</a>. It turns a
      natural-language question into a search of the site's checked data and
      writes the short answer at the top.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The AI doesn't create or verify the underlying facts. Those come from the
      dataset. If Ask disappeared tomorrow, the same checked information would
      still be there.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That distinction matters here. The whole point is knowing where an answer
      came from and when someone last checked it.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/changed/">What's changed, and when</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
